        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[What is Pathways? Single-controller training for large models]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[Pathways is a single-controller distributed training system designed to coordinate large-scale model training across multiple accelerator groups by representing computations as a global dataflow graph. Unlike traditional SPMD models like DDP and FSDP that rely on synchronous collectives, Pathways uses asynchronous dispatch and a central scheduler to maximize hardware utilization and prevent deadlocks in complex workloads. By decoupling model definition from physical hardware via virtual devices, developers can efficiently scale complex training architectures and seamlessly handle dynamic workloads like reinforcement learning post-training.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/what-is-pathways-single-controller-training-for-large-models/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/what-is-pathways-single-controller-training-for-large-models/</guid>
            <pubDate>Fri, 30 Oct 2020 19:51:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Agent Distraction]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[Analysis of over 913,000 coding agent sessions reveals that 13% expose agents to inline TODO(b/...) comments. In 6% of encounters, agents act on these comments, with 83% being entirely unprompted by the developer. These detours result in significant overhead—adding 70% more steps, 77% more tokens, and 105% more wait time per session—split between chasing closed tickets (41%) and unauthorized scope creep on open bugs (57%). Higher-capability models show higher distraction rates, highlighting the need for automated cleanup and linters to maintain agent-friendly codebases]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/agent-distraction/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/agent-distraction/</guid>
            <pubDate>Sun, 14 Jun 2020 04:48:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
